Before: input #day had value presumably something like "2026-11-20"? Not explicitly stated but likely yes. After: they saw bbox same, but assertion expects day value "#appointment" (maybe hidden input?) value ["2026-11-23"] but observed ["2026-11-21"].

Thus ArrowRight moved by +1 day ignoring weekend disabling logic.

So the issue: The keyboard handling in pikaday.js does not respect disableDayFn when adjusting via arrow keys. It just adds/subtracts days without checking if resulting day is disabled (weekend). Need to modify _onKeyChange or adjustDate method to skip disabled days.

Specifically, in _onKeyChange, cases 37 (left) and 39 (right) call self.adjustDate('subtract', 1) or self.adjustDate('add', 1). adjustDate simply adds/subtracts days via setDate(newDay). setDate will then apply min/max constraints but not disableDayFn. Actually setDate calls this._o.minDate/maxDate checks but does not call disableDayFn. There's also a check in render for disabled days but not in selection.

Thus we need to adjust adjustDate to skip disabled days according to disableDayFn (and also min/max). Or better: modify adjustDate to loop until date is not disabled (if disableDayFn defined) and within min/max.

Given the disableDayFn is set to date => date.getDay() === 0 || date.getDay() === 6 (i.e., weekend). So we need to skip weekends.

Implementation approach: In adjustDate, after computing newDay, while (disableDayFn && disableDayFn(newDay)) { adjust further in same direction }. Also need to respect min/max boundaries to avoid infinite loop.

Alternatively, modify _onKeyChange to call a custom method that steps until valid date.

Simpler: modify adjustDate to accept a skipDisabled flag? But we can just incorporate inside adjustDate: after computing newDay, if disableDayFn defined, loop while newDay is disabled and within bounds, adjust again.

